<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pataliputra</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pataliputra, the site of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, was an ancient city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It served as the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>, providing a symbolic and administrative centre for its vast territory. It remained an important commercial and administrative city under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pataliputra.jpg" alt="Pataliputra" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pyule · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pataliputra6.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Urban centres including <b>Pataliputra</b>, Ujjain, and Vaishali served as commercial and administrative hubs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…BCE, wrote of the Yavanas besieging Saketa and Madhyamika, and the Yuga Purana prophesies that they would reach <b>Pataliputra</b> and cast down its mud walls before civil war called them home; whether such a campaign took place is disputed, and the Hathigumpha inscription has King Kharavela of Kalinga…</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The period saw what archaeologists call the second urbanisation of India: fortified towns such as Rajagriha, Champa and later <b>Pataliputra</b> grew along rivers and trade routes, the fine Northern Black Polished Ware spread as a luxury pottery, and silver punch-marked coins came into use.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Kingdom of Magadha · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Rabatak inscription claims that Kanishka's authority reached cities such as Saketa, Kausambi, <b>Pataliputra</b> and Champa, though how far this meant direct rule rather than recognition is uncertain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pataliputra</b> (modern Patna), already the Nanda capital, served as the imperial centre, which Megasthenes described as a vast city ringed by a timber palisade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Pala kings ruled as hereditary monarchs with the imperial titles Parameshvara, Paramabhattaraka and Maharajadhiraja, and they moved between victory camps, jayaskandhavara, at <b>Pataliputra</b>, Mudgagiri (Monghyr) and later Ramavati, founded by Ramapala, rather than holding a single fixed capital.</blockquote><a href="